</example><!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
<!--Q1-->
<exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
    <blockquote>
      <para>Use the following information to answer questions 1 - 5. A 450-gallon tank full of water is draining at a rate of 20 gallons per minute.</para>
    </blockquote>
    <para>Write an equation that represents the relationship between the gallons of water in the tank and hours the tank
        has
        been draining.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(a=450-1,200h\) where \(h\) is time in hours.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->

<!--Q2-->
<exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>Write an equation that represents the relationship between the gallons of water in the tank and seconds the
        tank
        has been draining.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(a=450-\frac{s}{3}\) where \(s\) is time in seconds.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->

<!--Q3 new-->
<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0" data-wait-for-event="RFM1"/>

<exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>Graph each of your new equations.Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the equation that represents this scenario using the Desmos tool above. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
    <figure><media alt="Linear function."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/54873f473fb892340f99aa047fc492c3531f2c16"/></media></figure></solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->

<!--Q4-->
<exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>In what way are all of the graphs the same? In what way are they all different? Write down your answer. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here are some samples. </para>
    <list>
      <item>They all have the same \(y\)-intercept.
      </item>
      <item>They all decrease.
      </item>
      <item>The slopes are different.
      </item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->

<!--Q5-->
<exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>How would these graphs change if we used quarts of water instead of gallons? What would stay the same?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here are some sample samples:
      The graphs would still be lines with negative slopes that are different. They would have the same horizontal intercepts. The vertical intercepts would be different, but they would represent the same amount of water.
    </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->
